if_arp: the probing of an IPv4 address made as short as Linux's
NetworkManager makes it (docs/tessronos/09-network.md 9.3.1).

Why: before an address is used, NetBSD checks with ARP that no other
machine has it (RFC 5227: a wait of up to PROBE_WAIT, PROBE_NUM probes
PROBE_MIN to PROBE_MAX apart, ANNOUNCE_WAIT after the last), which with
the RFC's times in seconds keeps a new address from being used for 4 to
8 s after DHCP's ACK. NetworkManager's ARP checking (n-acd, the
ipv4.dad-timeout of 200 ms it uses by default) keeps the RFC's three
probes and its steps, each of the RFC's times scaled by the timeout over
7000 ms (n_acd_probe_config_set_timeout: PROBE_WAIT and PROBE_MIN
28.6 ms, PROBE_MAX and ANNOUNCE_WAIT 57.1 ms, at most 200 ms in all),
and the announcements that follow at the RFC's own times (ANNOUNCE_NUM
of 2, ANNOUNCE_INTERVAL of 2 s). The same here: ip_dad_timeout_ms (200)
scales the waits of the probing; ip_dad_count, the announcements and
the defence (DEFEND_INTERVAL) are not changed. A duplicate seen while
probing marks the address IN_IFF_DUPLICATED as before.

Applied by tools/netbsd/build.sh and tools/x64/netbsd/build.sh to a copy
of the file; NetBSD's tree is not changed.

--- a/sys/netinet/if_arp.c
+++ b/sys/netinet/if_arp.c
@@ -167,6 +167,23 @@
 };
 
 int ip_dad_count = PROBE_NUM;
+/*
+ * The probing of an address scaled to take at most this many
+ * milliseconds: RFC 5227's waits (PROBE_WAIT, PROBE_MIN, PROBE_MAX,
+ * ANNOUNCE_WAIT, in seconds) times ip_dad_timeout_ms / 7000, 7000 being
+ * the RFC's own total. The announcements after it keep the RFC's times.
+ */
+int ip_dad_timeout_ms = 200;
+
+/* The ticks of an RFC 5227 wait of sec seconds, scaled; at least one */
+static int
+arp_dad_ticks(int sec)
+{
+	int64_t t;
+
+	t = ((int64_t)sec * ip_dad_timeout_ms * hz + 7000 - 1) / 7000;
+	return (t < 1) ? 1 : (int)t;
+}
 #ifdef ARP_DEBUG
 int arp_debug = 1;
 #else
@@ -1626,7 +1643,7 @@
 	ARPLOG(LOG_DEBUG, "%s: starting DAD for %s\n", if_name(ifa->ifa_ifp),
 	    ARPLOGADDR(&ia->ia_addr.sin_addr));
 
-	arp_dad_starttimer(dp, cprng_fast32() % (PROBE_WAIT * hz));
+	arp_dad_starttimer(dp, cprng_fast32() % arp_dad_ticks(PROBE_WAIT));
 
 	mutex_exit(&arp_dad_lock);
 }
